<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kamarupa</h1><p class="st-meta">350 CE – 1140 CE (approx.) · capital Pragjyotishpura</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The ancient kingdom of Assam in the Brahmaputra valley, ruled by three successive dynasties for some 800 years.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Kamarupa, known in the epics as Pragjyotisha, emerged as a state in the mid-4th century under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pushyavarman">Pushyavarman</a>, founder of the Varman dynasty. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prayagraj">Allahabad</a> pillar inscription lists it among frontier kingdoms paying tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samudragupta">Samudragupta</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a>. Its kings claimed descent from the mythical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narakasura">Naraka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagadatta">Bhagadatta</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The greatest Varman king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhaskaravarman">Bhaskaravarman</a>, allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harsha</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/pushyabhuti-dynasty">Pushyabhuti dynasty</a> and received the Chinese pilgrim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> around 643. After him the Mlechchha dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salastambha">Salastambha</a> ruled from Haruppeshvara, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tezpur">Tezpur</a>, followed by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/pala-empire">Pala dynasty</a> of Kamarupa. Kings made land grants to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> recorded on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> plates; the kingdom traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, aloe wood and elephants, and the goddess shrine at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakhya-temple">Kamakhya</a> became a major centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaktism">Shakta</a> worship.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Kamarupa fragmented in the 12th century after the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/pala-empire">Pala dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> installed its general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaidyadeva">Vaidyadeva</a> as ruler. Smaller successor states, among them Kamata, took its place, and in the 13th century the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ahom-kingdom">Ahom kingdom</a> rose in the upper valley.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kamarupa.jpg" alt="Kamarupa" width="320" height="319"><figcaption>Image: Chaipau · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kamarupa_map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>350 CE – 1140 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pragjyotishpura</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Pushyavarman, Bhaskaravarman, Ratnapala</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism, Shaktism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sanskrit, Kamarupi Prakrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a>, <a href="/countries/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>, <a href="/countries/bhutan">Bhutan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Pragjyotisha, Kingdom of Kamarupa, Varman dynasty of Kamarupa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=745&amp;focus=kamarupa" aria-label="Kamarupa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7085 1222 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kamarupa lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7405 1478L7348 1548M7405 1478L7362 1550M7405 1478L7253 1492"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7348 1548h0M7362 1550h0M7253 1492h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7405 1478h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7405 1478h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=745&amp;focus=kamarupa">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
